86311942 :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the limit.\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x+5}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311944 :question/problem "![](images/52/52c11d9ff3afeef74c579d4cb2c5448c9f5b77b7d6569535214bb67408276026.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\sqrt{\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{f(x)}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311948 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{5x}}{5-x}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311949 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 8}\\frac{8-\\sqrt{8x}}{x-8}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311950 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-x-6}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311952 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311953 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 64}\\frac{x-64}{\\sqrt{4x}-16}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311954 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{3x-\\sqrt{27x}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311958 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{z\\to 6}\\frac{z-6}{\\sqrt{3z-2}-4}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314717 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314717 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${x}^{2}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186314718 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+2)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314718 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+2)=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186314719 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{x/4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}-2)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314719 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x/4}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x/4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{x/4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}-2)=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186314739 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314739 :question/worked-solution "The numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with \n\n$$\n|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $x$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ Since the denominator grows much faster than the (bounded) numerator, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/e1/e164233abcdf9062fd856a100fb7b792912fa030746a63f01ca7fd6683bf7f09.png)"] [17592186314740 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314740 :question/worked-solution "The numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with \n\n$$\n|4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 4.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator ${x}^{2}$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ Since the denominator grows much faster than the (bounded) numerator, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/fd/fd6dbbe8be077ed2eda955802f112540aa8aea73c5c65bae4f0f034176bbdccd.png)"] [17592186314741 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x+{x}^{2}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314741 :question/worked-solution "The numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with \n\n$$\n|-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 2.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $4x+{x}^{2}$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ Since the denominator grows much faster than the (bounded) numerator, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x+{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x+{x}^{2}},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/dd/dd869f9140a12c19a0672963c064a4581767a3e39fac8c49b76b6e106755080f.png)"] [17592186314754 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}(2\\sqrt{3}x+6\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314771 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }(\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x+1))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314771 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x+1):$\n\n![](images/e4/e441953c2cefc6f817a64cb7ce0ebce53164ebb4d3974ae871b14d57450b5167.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x+1)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x+1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }(\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x+1))=\\pi -\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$"] [17592186314776 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }2(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x+5))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314776 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x+5):$ \n\n![](images/25/25cea33f2fc3a3bcdbc10ca03f93bd6059eb7d0a7dfcda5eb59f6f671387aea3.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x+5)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x+5)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }2(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x+5))=2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\pi .\n$$"] [17592186314782 :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{4{x}^{6}-5x}=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314787 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}sin\\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314789 :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|-12x+5|}{4x+3}=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314797 :question/problem "If $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=10$ and $g(10)=5,$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {10}^{+}}\\frac{g(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡x}{3g(10)}=\n$$\n {{field-1}}."] [17592186314799 :question/problem "If $g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=5$ and $g(5)=5,$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{g(x)-{x}^{2}}{4x}=\n$$\n {{field-1}}."] [17592186314800 :question/problem "If $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=10$ and $h(10)=15,$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {10}^{-}}(xh(x)-5h(10))=\n$$\n {{field-1}}."] [17592186314802 :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4x}{{x}^{2}+x+5}=$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314803 :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{9}-6{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{3}+10x}=$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314804 :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}-2}{5{x}^{2}-1}=$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314822 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\left(-\\frac{21\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}\\right)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314839 :question/problem "Find the left- and right-sided limits at $x=-2$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x} & \\text{ for }\\,x<-2 \\\\ {e}^{2+x}+3 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge -2.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}},$\\,$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186314855 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314856 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{5}⁡x}{4{x}^{4}}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314882 :question/problem "Find the left-sided limit at $x=\\pi $ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}x+\\pi  & \\text{ for }\\,x<\\pi  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge \\pi .\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314883 :question/problem "Find the right-sided limit at $x=8$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}-3x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 8 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{x}+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>8.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314884 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}(x+2)^{3}+7 & \\text{ for }\\,x<-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-4}-1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 4.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186314885 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (x+1))-5 & \\text{ for }\\,x<-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-2}+(x-2)^{2}-3 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 2.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186314893 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}3sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)+2 & \\text{ for }\\,x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{7x+2}-1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 2.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186315085 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the left-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function has three discontinuities: $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3.$\n\n- At $x=-2$ the function is not left-continuous because it's undefined at this point. $\\,\\times $\n\n- At $x=1$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=2=f(1).\\,✓\n$$\n\n- At $x=3$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=0=f(3).\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, from **left to right**, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous but is right-continuous at $x=1$ and $x=3.$"] [17592186315086 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the right-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function has three discontinuities: $x=-3$, $x=-1,$ and $x=1.$\n\n- At $x=-3$ the function is right-continuous because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{+}}f(x)=-2=f(-3).\\,✓\n$$\n\n- At $x=-1$ the function is not right-continuous because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}f(x)=0\\ne -2=f(-1).\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- At $x=1$ the function is right-continuous because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=0=f(1).\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, from **left to right**, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous but is right-continuous at\n$x=-3$ and $x=1.$"] [17592186315095 :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-1,2]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-1,2]$ except at the point $x=1,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=6\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-1,2]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-1,2].$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-1,2]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-1,2].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f,g,$ and $h.$\""] [17592186315096 :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ except at the point $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{-}}g(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{+}}g(x)=0\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f,g,$ and $h.$\""] [17592186315097 :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ except at the point $x=1,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=0\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ since the one-sided limits at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{-}}g(x)=+\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{+}}g(x)=-\\infty \n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $h$ only.\""] [17592186315098 :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2]$ since the one-sided limits at $x=1$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=+\\infty \n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,2].$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,2]$ except at the point $x=1,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x)=3,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x)=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\""] [17592186315108 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the right-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function has three discontinuities: $x=-1$ and $x=3.$\n\n- At $x=-1$ the function is right-continuous because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}f(x)=1=f(-1).\\,✓\n$$\n\n- At $x=3$ the function is not right-continuous because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(3).\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous but is right-continuous at $x=-1.$"] [17592186315110 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=6$ and $x=2,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {6}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {6}^{+}}f(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=2\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{-}}g(x)=+\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{+}}g(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=…,-4,-2,0,2,4,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/b9/b9ffe76f612fdc577d64526ebc3322b81a87ec3856040c1c8edccb1be3b6fe5d.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $h$ only.\""] [17592186317158 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-5,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=…,-2,-1,0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[-5,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/78/788b349598dcabebf92609183b3c48851d75bb11e22efff881a208e58be510b9.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-5,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-5,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=-4$ and $x=-1,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{4}^{-}}g(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to -{4}^{+}}g(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}g(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}g(x)=2\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-5,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{-}}h(x)=+\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{+}}h(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $g$ only.\""]}
